FG-021: the inline PDF import asks for a password when the file is encrypted (pdf-docx, pdf-pptx).

xpdf_ImportFromFile's WebAssembly branch handed poppler only the password the caller gave in the
Password media-descriptor property and ignored the interaction handler, so an encrypted PDF
loaded through LibreOfficeKit (which raises password requests through its handler, not through
that property) failed with "Incorrect password" (PDFDoc errorCode 4, errEncrypted) and no request.
Stock LibreOffice opens the same file because its subprocess branch asks the handler.

(pdf-docx and pdf-pptx get different sets of the pdfimport patches, so the hunks avoid the lines those
sets change differently; both module series were replayed to check that this applies to each.)

pdfi_inline_last_error() reports the last PDFDoc's error code. When poppler reports errEncrypted and
what was given did not open the file, the import asks the handler through pdfi::getPassword (a
DocumentPasswordRequest2, the same request the subprocess branch makes) and tries again; a host with no
handler, or a handler that declines, ends the load as before. A password given up front is not asked
for again. A failed open writes no output, so the same output files serve every attempt. The password
is tried as UTF-8 (PDF 2.0) and, when it differs and fits, as Latin-1 (older revisions); ASCII is
identical. The password is never logged.

The request names the document the host LOADED (the URL PDFIRawAdaptor::parse receives), not the temp file
xpdf_ImportFromStream copies the stream to: LibreOfficeKit matches a password request to its load by that
name, and the bridge answers only a request that names its load.

WAF review of the rebased stack (#659), three changes: (1) the loop that asks the handler has a hard cap, kMaxHandlerRequests = 3, so it ends
whatever the host's handler or the bridge's decline policy does; (2) g_inlineDocError is set to -1 as the FIRST statement of
pdfi_process_pdf_inline, so a throw in the GlobalParams block cannot leave a stale errEncrypted that turns an unrelated failure into a prompt;
(3) the Latin-1 candidate uses the *_ERROR conversion flags, so "fits" means converts without loss (the old flags substituted '?').

diff --git a/sdext/source/pdfimport/wrapper/wrapper.cxx b/sdext/source/pdfimport/wrapper/wrapper.cxx
index 55ef2b6..9044022 100644
--- a/sdext/source/pdfimport/wrapper/wrapper.cxx
+++ b/sdext/source/pdfimport/wrapper/wrapper.cxx
@@ -1981,6 +1981,8 @@ extern "C" bool pdfi_process_pdf_inline(
     bool skipImages,
     void (*pHeartbeat)(void* pCtx, int nPage, int nPages),
     void* pHeartbeatCtx);
+// errEncrypted (poppler ErrorCodes.h) is 4; 0 means the last PDFDoc opened.
+extern "C" int pdfi_inline_last_error();
 
 // FG-142: the render phase reports page progress (and a heartbeat on a slow
 // page) through the sink, which owns the load status indicator.
@@ -1989,6 +1991,12 @@ static void lcl_renderHeartbeat(void* pCtx, int nPage, int nPages)
     static_cast<ContentSink*>(pCtx)->renderHeartbeat(nPage, nPages);
 }
 
+// The URL the document was LOADED from, set by PDFIRawAdaptor::parse before it imports. xpdf_ImportFromStream copies
+// the stream to a temp file, so rURL below names the temp file; a password request must name the document the host
+// loaded (LibreOfficeKit matches the request to its load by that name).
+static OUString g_pdfiLoadURL;
+void pdfi_setDocumentName(const OUString& rLoadURL) { g_pdfiLoadURL = rLoadURL; }
+
 bool xpdf_ImportFromFile(const OUString& rURL,
                          const ContentSinkSharedPtr& rSink,
                          const uno::Reference<task::XInteractionHandler>& xIHdl,
@@ -2005,12 +2013,11 @@ bool xpdf_ImportFromFile(const OUString& rURL,
         return false;
     }
     OUString aDocName( rURL.copy( rURL.lastIndexOf( '/' )+1 ) );
+    const OUString aRequestName = g_pdfiLoadURL.isEmpty() ? aDocName : g_pdfiLoadURL;
 
     // In 25.8, checkEncryption was removed (tdf#55425) - encryption is
     // handled by poppler directly during processing.
     OUString aPwd( rPwd );
-    (void)xIHdl;
-    (void)aDocName;
 
     SAL_INFO("sdext.pdfimport", "WASM: Starting inline PDF import for " << aSysUPath);
 
@@ -2066,23 +2073,71 @@ bool xpdf_ImportFromFile(const OUString& rURL,
 
     // Convert strings for Poppler
     OString aPDFPath = OUStringToOString(aSysUPath, osl_getThreadTextEncoding());
-    OString aPwdStr;
-    if (!aPwd.isEmpty())
-        aPwdStr = OUStringToOString(aPwd, RTL_TEXTENCODING_ISO_8859_1);
-
     bool bSkipImages = (rFilterOptions == "SkipImages");
 
-    // Call the C function that does the actual Poppler processing
-    // Pass password if provided (poppler handles encryption directly in 25.8)
-    bool bRet = pdfi_process_pdf_inline(
-        aPDFPath.getStr(),
-        !aPwd.isEmpty() ? aPwdStr.getStr() : nullptr,
-        !aPwd.isEmpty() ? aPwdStr.getStr() : nullptr,
-        pTextFile,
-        pBinaryFile,
-        bSkipImages,
-        &lcl_renderHeartbeat,
-        rSink.get());
+    // Call the C function that does the actual Poppler processing, with the password the caller
+    // gave (the Password media-descriptor property). If poppler says the file is encrypted and that
+    // did not open it, ask the interaction handler for a password, as the subprocess branch above
+    // does (a host with no handler, or one that declines, ends the load). A failed open writes no
+    // output, so the same output files serve every attempt. The password is never logged.
+    constexpr int kErrEncrypted = 4;
+    // A hard stop on how often the handler is asked: the loop must end whatever the host's handler or the bridge's decline policy does.
+    constexpr int kMaxHandlerRequests = 3;
+    int nHandlerRequests = 0;
+    const bool bPasswordOnEntry = !aPwd.isEmpty();
+    bool bFirstTry = true;
+    bool bRet = false;
+    for (;;)
+    {
+        // PDF 2.0 (AES-256) takes the password as UTF-8, older revisions as Latin-1/PDFDocEncoding: try
+        // UTF-8 first and, only if that differs and converts without loss (no '?' substituted), Latin-1 as well. ASCII is identical in both.
+        std::vector<OString> aCandidates;
+        if (!aPwd.isEmpty())
+        {
+            aCandidates.push_back(OUStringToOString(aPwd, RTL_TEXTENCODING_UTF8));
+            OString aLatin1;
+            if (aPwd.convertToString(&aLatin1, RTL_TEXTENCODING_ISO_8859_1,
+                                    RTL_UNICODETOTEXT_FLAGS_UNDEFINED_ERROR | RTL_UNICODETOTEXT_FLAGS_INVALID_ERROR)
+                && aLatin1 != aCandidates[0])
+                aCandidates.push_back(aLatin1);
+        }
+        else
+            aCandidates.push_back(OString());
+
+        for (const OString& rCand : aCandidates)
+        {
+            bRet = pdfi_process_pdf_inline(
+                aPDFPath.getStr(),
+                !rCand.isEmpty() ? rCand.getStr() : nullptr,
+                !rCand.isEmpty() ? rCand.getStr() : nullptr,
+                pTextFile,
+                pBinaryFile,
+                bSkipImages,
+                &lcl_renderHeartbeat,
+                rSink.get());
+            if (bRet || pdfi_inline_last_error() != kErrEncrypted)
+                break;
+        }
+        if (bRet || pdfi_inline_last_error() != kErrEncrypted)
+            break;
+        // encrypted, and what we have did not open it
+        if (!xIHdl.is() || (bPasswordOnEntry && bFirstTry))
+            break;   // no one to ask, or a password given up front is not asked for again
+        if (nHandlerRequests >= kMaxHandlerRequests)
+        {
+            SAL_INFO("sdext.pdfimport", "the handler was asked " << nHandlerRequests << " times for the encrypted PDF: giving up");
+            break;
+        }
+        ++nHandlerRequests;
+        OUString aEntered;
+        if (!pdfi::getPassword(xIHdl, aEntered, bFirstTry, aRequestName))
+        {
+            SAL_INFO("sdext.pdfimport", "no password was given for the encrypted PDF");
+            break;
+        }
+        aPwd = aEntered;
+        bFirstTry = false;
+    }
 
     // Close output files
     fclose(pTextFile);
